{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"pooled-live-query-and-flat-change-tracking-oracle-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled live query and flat change tracking oracle review"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reviewed-state-and-claim","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed state and claim"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"84b828c7e"},{"type":"text","value":", merged with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":" at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18abceee4"},{"type":"text","value":". This record reviews\nthe Git tree that contains it; the final commit or pull request identifies that\ntree."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The pooled live query oracle claims that a live query filtered only by\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq(field, literal)"},{"type":"text","value":" on one eager, non-persisted source, served from an\nequality partition, publishes what its live-query Collection would: the same\nrows in key order, values, and status. That holds through sync transactions,\noptimistic writes confirmed or rolled back, peer mounts and unmounts, and\nsource cleanup and restart. The claim excludes on-demand and persisted\nsources, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DbClient"},{"type":"text","value":" hydration, Suspense, and every clause beyond "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":"\nconjuncts, which keep the live-query Collection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The flat change tracking oracle claims that, for a row whose own fields are\nall primitives or functions with a plain or null prototype and no symbol\nkeys, the flat tracker reports the same change set as the draft proxy and an\nindependent model, with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" equal. Nested values, Dates, Maps, Sets,\nclass instances, and symbol keys are outside it, except that they must fall\nback to the proxy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both oracles use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mockSyncCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" or plain rows; neither claims a\nreal sync adapter's behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"red-and-green-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RED and GREEN evidence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutants ran through each oracle file alone on the reviewed tree. Each file was\nrestored after each run. Every outcome below is an assertion failure."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tests failed"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partition ignores a row's previous group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Groups keep rows in arrival order"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Literals and fields compared without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" normalization"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partition skips the source's initial state"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"View reports the source's status after cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partition never terminates on cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partition keys groups by the first field only"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update published as delete then insert"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 9 (0 of 7 before payload checks)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update carries a stale "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"previousValue"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 9 (0 of 7 before)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update carries the old row as its value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 9 (0 of 7 before)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Within-group updates dropped"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partition built on a cleaned-up source starts terminal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 9 (0 of 7 before)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Frozen peers drop pending optimistic rows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 9 (0 of 7 before)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat diff with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"!=="}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat diff with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Object.is"},{"type":"text","value":" alone"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat diff without deletions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Draft proxy without the added-field revert fix"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pinned history and both campaigns"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat diff ignores whether the row owns a field"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat drafts edit the rows in place"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assigned objects not detached"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1 of 17 (detach witness)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Frozen rows sent to the proxy"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Proxy ignores accessors defined in the callback"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Proxy reports a field defined back to its own value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Proxy accepts a getter-only field's own value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Proxy reports a written hidden field's delete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 17"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat compares a hidden object field by identity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 of 17"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every pooled and flat mutant above fails its pinned history and both\ncampaigns. The pooled grammar delivers initial rows in key order or in\nreverse and weights a pending insert most peers see before cleanup; the flat\ngrammar weights runs that write and delete a hidden field, write an equal\nobject over a hidden object field, assign a getter-only field its own value, and write the opposite-signed zero\nover each zero field. Before that weighting, arrival order and frozen optimistic rows\nescaped about one random campaign in three, and the three descriptor\nmutants escaped both campaigns. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Object.is"},{"type":"text","value":"-alone flat mutant escaped about one random campaign in two\nuntil the zero-flip run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The pooled oracle found that a pooled view followed its source's status after\ncleanup instead of entering the live query's terminal error; the repair makes\nthe partition terminate. The flat oracle found that the draft proxy dropped a\nfield added as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" when another field reverted, on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" as well; the\nrepair treats a field the original lacks as changed. The grammar now weights\nthat run, so both campaigns also kill the unrepaired proxy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The loss audit then widened both oracles. The pooled granular observer had\nbeen checked by key membership only, so the payload and lifecycle mutants\nmarked \"0 of 7 before\" survived it. The flat grammar gained frozen rows,\nnon-enumerable fields, accessor and data "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineProperty"},{"type":"text","value":", stored drafts, and\nthrowing callbacks. Four shapes split the trackers, three of them on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":"'s\nproxy too. The adopted rule is that defining a field acts as assigning it,\nand that a non-enumerable field is row data only once the callback writes\nit. The proxy now records accessors and data defines through the assignment\npath, rejects a getter-only write of its own value, and ignores the delete of\na hidden field it wrote. The flat tracker now compares a hidden object field\nby contents."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local-only direct writes are compared with a local-only Collection whose\nhandlers resolve, across mixed multi-key batches, failing batches, schema\nrejection, and every fallback for insert, update, and delete. Mutants that\nignore handler types, drop the pending or persisting check, drop the whole\ntransaction check, run before the ambient branch, write only the first\nmutation, or never complete the transaction fail 4, 4, 4, 8, 12, 1, and 9\ntests."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two shared conformance scenarios, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq-filter-rows"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq-filter-peers"},{"type":"text","value":", run\nthe pooled path under React, Vue, Solid, Svelte, and Angular. A partition that\nignores a row's previous group fails both under every adapter. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq-filter-peers"},{"type":"text","value":" also checks the\nsource's public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscriberCount"},{"type":"text","value":": an adapter that declares "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pooledEqFilters"},{"type":"text","value":"\nmust share one subscription for two queries on the same fields. Disabling pooling or using one partition per query fails it under React;\nbefore this check the first passed. Each adapter's driver declares\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pooledEqFilters"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The loss audit found that a partition released its source one second after\nits last listener, whatever "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":" its views had. A focused release-timing\ntest, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pooled-live-query-gc.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", now compares pooled and live-query\nCollection release times. A fixed delay failed 5 of 10 cases, a missing 50 ms\nfloor for unsubscribed queries 1, a last-"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":"-wins rule 1, and releasing\nat "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":" 0 2. Release timing is resource lifetime, so the publication\noracle does not observe it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooling later admitted residual conjuncts: any "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":" conjunct that reads\nonly the query's own row, beside at least one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":", is evaluated per view\nwith the compiler's evaluator. Peers may add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"not(eq(g, literal))"},{"type":"text","value":", which the\nmodel evaluates itself, and a pinned history moves rows in and out of a view\nwithin one group. A view that ignores the residual, reports unfiltered\nentries, or sends a row entering or leaving it as an update fails the\npinned history and both campaigns. Hiding part of a group makes some\nearlier histories rarer, so in one full run the arrival-order,\nnormalization, delete-plus-insert, and dropped in-group update mutants\nescaped the random campaign; the fixed campaign and their pinned histories\nstill kill each. Peers' literals were weighted toward the normalized values\nto keep the normalization mutant in the fixed campaign."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pooled-live-query-oracle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pooled live query oracle"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 Contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" operand rules come from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"; the pooled boundary and the terminal-error rule come from the live-query architecture document's pooled section and cleanup law. The opening prose lists the omissions."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 Independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedKeys"},{"type":"text","value":" uses a local "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" over plain values. Order, values, and status come from a live-query Collection, which compiles a D2 pipeline and does not use the partition."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Contract, model, grammar, driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 Generated-history controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Reconstruction: every pinned history uses only domain values and step kinds. Ablation, run per axis on the campaigns with pinned cases skipped: without Dates, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" the normalization mutant survives; without optimistic steps, frozen peers dropping optimistic rows survives; without mounts and unmounts, the terminal-at-creation mutant survives; without cleanup-restart, the status, termination, and both pending-cleanup mutants survive; without the second conjunct, first-field grouping survives. Range: at most four rows, three peers, eight steps, and ids 0 through 3; field values weight toward the literal so rows update within their group. Exclusion: an optimistic update to an equal value, an insert of an existing id, and an update or delete of a missing id are dropped."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 Production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createPooledLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createLiveQueryObserver"},{"type":"text","value":", the adapter seam, run in wholesale and granular mode; the conformance scenarios run through React's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":". Rows, keyed state, status, layout revision, and non-materialization are observed after every step, and granular changes are compared with a reference observer by type, key, value, and previous value, then replayed against the model."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 Checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Thirteen mutants, classified above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 Fixed/random replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"44_502_001"},{"type":"text","value":", an unseeded campaign, and a replay entry share one property and budget; the file is in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"test:oracles"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The model keeps source rows and, per mounted peer, the frozen keys at cleanup. The pinned cleanup history distinguishes a frozen peer from one mounted after the restart."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Equality partition, partition group, and pooled live query are glossary terms; a peer is one mounted pooled live query."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withOracleCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":" releases observers, references, and the source and keeps the check failure."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 Independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The live-query Collection is the second formulation for order, values, and status."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 Review evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record; the coverage map links it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013 Reusable boundary law"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Normalization is rejected by the Date history, arrival order by the key-order history, and following the source's status by the cleanup history."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. The claim is limited to the mock source's sync transactions and lifecycle."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"flat-change-tracking-oracle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat change tracking oracle"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 Contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The change-set rules come from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/proxy.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getChanges"},{"type":"text","value":" contract: changed fields with their final value and deleted fields as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 Independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedChanges"},{"type":"text","value":" folds the operations over a plain copy and does not import either tracker."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Contract, model, grammar, driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 Generated-history controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Reconstruction: every pinned history uses domain values and operations. Ablation: removing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":", reverts, or deletions each loses a mutant. Range: one to three rows, three fields plus one added and one non-enumerable field, frozen or not, up to six operations per row including data and accessor defines, stored drafts, and a throw. Exclusion: none in generation; non-flat rows are rejected by the fallback witness."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 Production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withFlatChangeTracking"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withArrayChangeTracking"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withChangeTracking"},{"type":"text","value":" run the same callbacks; the result change sets are observed. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.update"},{"type":"text","value":" selects between them."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 Checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Thirteen mutants, classified above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 Fixed/random replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"44_502_101"},{"type":"text","value":", an unseeded campaign, and a replay entry; the file is in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"test:oracles"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. The model recomputes from the operations."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Draft, change set, and revert follow the proxy's terms."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. The oracle holds no resources."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 Independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The draft proxy is the second formulation."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 Review evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record; the coverage map links it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013 Reusable boundary law"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"!=="},{"type":"text","value":" is rejected by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" history, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Object.is"},{"type":"text","value":" by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" history, and missing deletions by the deleted-field history."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. No provider is involved."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"open-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open work"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte's suite reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" from its built "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dist"},{"type":"text","value":", so a mutant\nmust type-check and be rebuilt before Svelte can observe it."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"pooled-live-query-and-flat-change-tracking-oracle-review","text":"Pooled live query and flat change tracking oracle review","level":1},{"id":"reviewed-state-and-claim","text":"Reviewed state and claim","level":2},{"id":"red-and-green-evidence","text":"RED and GREEN evidence","level":2},{"id":"pooled-live-query-oracle","text":"Pooled live query oracle","level":2},{"id":"flat-change-tracking-oracle","text":"Flat change tracking oracle","level":2},{"id":"open-work","text":"Open work","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Pooled live query and flat change tracking oracle review\n\n## Reviewed state and claim\n\nBase: `84b828c7e`, merged with `origin/main` at `18abceee4`. This record reviews\nthe Git tree that contains it; the final commit or pull request identifies that\ntree.\n\nThe pooled live query oracle claims that a live query filtered only by\n`eq(field, literal)` on one eager, non-persisted source, served from an\nequality partition, publishes what its live-query Collection would: the same\nrows in key order, values, and status. That holds through sync transactions,\noptimistic writes confirmed or rolled back, peer mounts and unmounts, and\nsource cleanup and restart. The claim excludes on-demand and persisted\nsources, `DbClient` hydration, Suspense, and every clause beyond `eq`\nconjuncts, which keep the live-query Collection.\n\nThe flat change tracking oracle claims that, for a row whose own fields are\nall primitives or functions with a plain or null prototype and no symbol\nkeys, the flat tracker reports the same change set as the draft proxy and an\nindependent model, with `0` and `-0` equal. Nested values, Dates, Maps, Sets,\nclass instances, and symbol keys are outside it, except that they must fall\nback to the proxy.\n\nBoth oracles use `mockSyncCollectionOptions` or plain rows; neither claims a\nreal sync adapter's behavior.\n\n## RED and GREEN evidence\n\nMutants ran through each oracle file alone on the reviewed tree. Each file was\nrestored after each run. Every outcome below is an assertion failure.\n\n| Mutant | Oracle | Tests failed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Partition ignores a row's previous group | Pooled | 6 of 9 |\n| Groups keep rows in arrival order | Pooled | 3 of 9 |\n| Literals and fields compared without `eq` normalization | Pooled | 4 of 9 |\n| Partition skips the source's initial state | Pooled | 8 of 9 |\n| View reports the source's status after cleanup | Pooled | 4 of 9 |\n| Partition never terminates on cleanup | Pooled | 4 of 9 |\n| Partition keys groups by the first field only | Pooled | 5 of 9 |\n| Update published as delete then insert | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before payload checks) |\n| Update carries a stale `previousValue` | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Update carries the old row as its value | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Within-group updates dropped | Pooled | 3 of 9 |\n| Partition built on a cleaned-up source starts terminal | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Frozen peers drop pending optimistic rows | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Flat diff with `!==` | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Flat diff with `Object.is` alone | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Flat diff without deletions | Flat | 5 of 17 |\n| Draft proxy without the added-field revert fix | Flat | pinned history and both campaigns |\n| Flat diff ignores whether the row owns a field | Flat | 4 of 17 |\n| Flat drafts edit the rows in place | Flat | 9 of 17 |\n| Assigned objects not detached | Flat | 1 of 17 (detach witness) |\n| Frozen rows sent to the proxy | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Proxy ignores accessors defined in the callback | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Proxy reports a field defined back to its own value | Flat | 4 of 17 |\n| Proxy accepts a getter-only field's own value | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Proxy reports a written hidden field's delete | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Flat compares a hidden object field by identity | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n\nEvery pooled and flat mutant above fails its pinned history and both\ncampaigns. The pooled grammar delivers initial rows in key order or in\nreverse and weights a pending insert most peers see before cleanup; the flat\ngrammar weights runs that write and delete a hidden field, write an equal\nobject over a hidden object field, assign a getter-only field its own value, and write the opposite-signed zero\nover each zero field. Before that weighting, arrival order and frozen optimistic rows\nescaped about one random campaign in three, and the three descriptor\nmutants escaped both campaigns. The `Object.is`-alone flat mutant escaped about one random campaign in two\nuntil the zero-flip run.\n\nThe pooled oracle found that a pooled view followed its source's status after\ncleanup instead of entering the live query's terminal error; the repair makes\nthe partition terminate. The flat oracle found that the draft proxy dropped a\nfield added as `undefined` when another field reverted, on `main` as well; the\nrepair treats a field the original lacks as changed. The grammar now weights\nthat run, so both campaigns also kill the unrepaired proxy.\n\nThe loss audit then widened both oracles. The pooled granular observer had\nbeen checked by key membership only, so the payload and lifecycle mutants\nmarked \"0 of 7 before\" survived it. The flat grammar gained frozen rows,\nnon-enumerable fields, accessor and data `defineProperty`, stored drafts, and\nthrowing callbacks. Four shapes split the trackers, three of them on `main`'s\nproxy too. The adopted rule is that defining a field acts as assigning it,\nand that a non-enumerable field is row data only once the callback writes\nit. The proxy now records accessors and data defines through the assignment\npath, rejects a getter-only write of its own value, and ignores the delete of\na hidden field it wrote. The flat tracker now compares a hidden object field\nby contents.\n\nLocal-only direct writes are compared with a local-only Collection whose\nhandlers resolve, across mixed multi-key batches, failing batches, schema\nrejection, and every fallback for insert, update, and delete. Mutants that\nignore handler types, drop the pending or persisting check, drop the whole\ntransaction check, run before the ambient branch, write only the first\nmutation, or never complete the transaction fail 4, 4, 4, 8, 12, 1, and 9\ntests.\n\nTwo shared conformance scenarios, `eq-filter-rows` and `eq-filter-peers`, run\nthe pooled path under React, Vue, Solid, Svelte, and Angular. A partition that\nignores a row's previous group fails both under every adapter. `eq-filter-peers` also checks the\nsource's public `subscriberCount`: an adapter that declares `pooledEqFilters`\nmust share one subscription for two queries on the same fields. Disabling pooling or using one partition per query fails it under React;\nbefore this check the first passed. Each adapter's driver declares\n`pooledEqFilters`.\n\nThe loss audit found that a partition released its source one second after\nits last listener, whatever `gcTime` its views had. A focused release-timing\ntest, `pooled-live-query-gc.test.ts`, now compares pooled and live-query\nCollection release times. A fixed delay failed 5 of 10 cases, a missing 50 ms\nfloor for unsubscribed queries 1, a last-`gcTime`-wins rule 1, and releasing\nat `gcTime` 0 2. Release timing is resource lifetime, so the publication\noracle does not observe it.\n\nPooling later admitted residual conjuncts: any `where` conjunct that reads\nonly the query's own row, beside at least one `eq`, is evaluated per view\nwith the compiler's evaluator. Peers may add `not(eq(g, literal))`, which the\nmodel evaluates itself, and a pinned history moves rows in and out of a view\nwithin one group. A view that ignores the residual, reports unfiltered\nentries, or sends a row entering or leaving it as an update fails the\npinned history and both campaigns. Hiding part of a group makes some\nearlier histories rarer, so in one full run the arrival-order,\nnormalization, delete-plus-insert, and dropped in-group update mutants\nescaped the random campaign; the fixed campaign and their pinned histories\nstill kill each. Peers' literals were weighted toward the normalized values\nto keep the normalization mutant in the fixed campaign.\n\n## Pooled live query oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass. The `eq` operand rules come from `src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts`; the pooled boundary and the terminal-error rule come from the live-query architecture document's pooled section and cleanup law. The opening prose lists the omissions. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedKeys` uses a local `eq` over plain values. Order, values, and status come from a live-query Collection, which compiles a D2 pipeline and does not use the partition. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. Contract, model, grammar, driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass. Reconstruction: every pinned history uses only domain values and step kinds. Ablation, run per axis on the campaigns with pinned cases skipped: without Dates, `NaN`, and `-0` the normalization mutant survives; without optimistic steps, frozen peers dropping optimistic rows survives; without mounts and unmounts, the terminal-at-creation mutant survives; without cleanup-restart, the status, termination, and both pending-cleanup mutants survive; without the second conjunct, first-field grouping survives. Range: at most four rows, three peers, eight steps, and ids 0 through 3; field values weight toward the literal so rows update within their group. Exclusion: an optimistic update to an equal value, an insert of an existing id, and an update or delete of a missing id are dropped. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. `createPooledLiveQuery` and `createLiveQueryObserver`, the adapter seam, run in wholesale and granular mode; the conformance scenarios run through React's `useLiveQuery`. Rows, keyed state, status, layout revision, and non-materialization are observed after every step, and granular changes are compared with a reference observer by type, key, value, and previous value, then replayed against the model. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. Thirteen mutants, classified above. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. Fixed seed `44_502_001`, an unseeded campaign, and a replay entry share one property and budget; the file is in `test:oracles`. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Pass. The model keeps source rows and, per mounted peer, the frozen keys at cleanup. The pinned cleanup history distinguishes a frozen peer from one mounted after the restart. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass. Equality partition, partition group, and pooled live query are glossary terms; a peer is one mounted pooled live query. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. `withOracleCleanup` releases observers, references, and the source and keeps the check failure. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Pass. The live-query Collection is the second formulation for order, values, and status. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record; the coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. Normalization is rejected by the Date history, arrival order by the key-order history, and following the source's status by the cleanup history. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. The claim is limited to the mock source's sync transactions and lifecycle. |\n\n## Flat change tracking oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass. The change-set rules come from `src/proxy.ts`'s `getChanges` contract: changed fields with their final value and deleted fields as `undefined`. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedChanges` folds the operations over a plain copy and does not import either tracker. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. Contract, model, grammar, driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass. Reconstruction: every pinned history uses domain values and operations. Ablation: removing `NaN`, `-0`, reverts, or deletions each loses a mutant. Range: one to three rows, three fields plus one added and one non-enumerable field, frozen or not, up to six operations per row including data and accessor defines, stored drafts, and a throw. Exclusion: none in generation; non-flat rows are rejected by the fallback witness. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. `withFlatChangeTracking`, `withArrayChangeTracking`, and `withChangeTracking` run the same callbacks; the result change sets are observed. `collection.update` selects between them. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. Thirteen mutants, classified above. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. Fixed seed `44_502_101`, an unseeded campaign, and a replay entry; the file is in `test:oracles`. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Not triggered. The model recomputes from the operations. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass. Draft, change set, and revert follow the proxy's terms. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Not triggered. The oracle holds no resources. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Pass. The draft proxy is the second formulation. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record; the coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. `!==` is rejected by the `NaN` history, `Object.is` by the `-0` history, and missing deletions by the deleted-field history. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. No provider is involved. |\n\n## Open work\n\n- Svelte's suite reads `@tanstack/db` from its built `dist`, so a mutant\n  must type-check and be rebuilt before Svelte can observe it.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Pooled live query and flat change tracking oracle review\n\n## Reviewed state and claim\n\nBase: `84b828c7e`, merged with `origin/main` at `18abceee4`. This record reviews\nthe Git tree that contains it; the final commit or pull request identifies that\ntree.\n\nThe pooled live query oracle claims that a live query filtered only by\n`eq(field, literal)` on one eager, non-persisted source, served from an\nequality partition, publishes what its live-query Collection would: the same\nrows in key order, values, and status. That holds through sync transactions,\noptimistic writes confirmed or rolled back, peer mounts and unmounts, and\nsource cleanup and restart. The claim excludes on-demand and persisted\nsources, `DbClient` hydration, Suspense, and every clause beyond `eq`\nconjuncts, which keep the live-query Collection.\n\nThe flat change tracking oracle claims that, for a row whose own fields are\nall primitives or functions with a plain or null prototype and no symbol\nkeys, the flat tracker reports the same change set as the draft proxy and an\nindependent model, with `0` and `-0` equal. Nested values, Dates, Maps, Sets,\nclass instances, and symbol keys are outside it, except that they must fall\nback to the proxy.\n\nBoth oracles use `mockSyncCollectionOptions` or plain rows; neither claims a\nreal sync adapter's behavior.\n\n## RED and GREEN evidence\n\nMutants ran through each oracle file alone on the reviewed tree. Each file was\nrestored after each run. Every outcome below is an assertion failure.\n\n| Mutant | Oracle | Tests failed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Partition ignores a row's previous group | Pooled | 6 of 9 |\n| Groups keep rows in arrival order | Pooled | 3 of 9 |\n| Literals and fields compared without `eq` normalization | Pooled | 4 of 9 |\n| Partition skips the source's initial state | Pooled | 8 of 9 |\n| View reports the source's status after cleanup | Pooled | 4 of 9 |\n| Partition never terminates on cleanup | Pooled | 4 of 9 |\n| Partition keys groups by the first field only | Pooled | 5 of 9 |\n| Update published as delete then insert | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before payload checks) |\n| Update carries a stale `previousValue` | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Update carries the old row as its value | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Within-group updates dropped | Pooled | 3 of 9 |\n| Partition built on a cleaned-up source starts terminal | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Frozen peers drop pending optimistic rows | Pooled | 3 of 9 (0 of 7 before) |\n| Flat diff with `!==` | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Flat diff with `Object.is` alone | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Flat diff without deletions | Flat | 5 of 17 |\n| Draft proxy without the added-field revert fix | Flat | pinned history and both campaigns |\n| Flat diff ignores whether the row owns a field | Flat | 4 of 17 |\n| Flat drafts edit the rows in place | Flat | 9 of 17 |\n| Assigned objects not detached | Flat | 1 of 17 (detach witness) |\n| Frozen rows sent to the proxy | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Proxy ignores accessors defined in the callback | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Proxy reports a field defined back to its own value | Flat | 4 of 17 |\n| Proxy accepts a getter-only field's own value | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Proxy reports a written hidden field's delete | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n| Flat compares a hidden object field by identity | Flat | 3 of 17 |\n\nEvery pooled and flat mutant above fails its pinned history and both\ncampaigns. The pooled grammar delivers initial rows in key order or in\nreverse and weights a pending insert most peers see before cleanup; the flat\ngrammar weights runs that write and delete a hidden field, write an equal\nobject over a hidden object field, assign a getter-only field its own value, and write the opposite-signed zero\nover each zero field. Before that weighting, arrival order and frozen optimistic rows\nescaped about one random campaign in three, and the three descriptor\nmutants escaped both campaigns. The `Object.is`-alone flat mutant escaped about one random campaign in two\nuntil the zero-flip run.\n\nThe pooled oracle found that a pooled view followed its source's status after\ncleanup instead of entering the live query's terminal error; the repair makes\nthe partition terminate. The flat oracle found that the draft proxy dropped a\nfield added as `undefined` when another field reverted, on `main` as well; the\nrepair treats a field the original lacks as changed. The grammar now weights\nthat run, so both campaigns also kill the unrepaired proxy.\n\nThe loss audit then widened both oracles. The pooled granular observer had\nbeen checked by key membership only, so the payload and lifecycle mutants\nmarked \"0 of 7 before\" survived it. The flat grammar gained frozen rows,\nnon-enumerable fields, accessor and data `defineProperty`, stored drafts, and\nthrowing callbacks. Four shapes split the trackers, three of them on `main`'s\nproxy too. The adopted rule is that defining a field acts as assigning it,\nand that a non-enumerable field is row data only once the callback writes\nit. The proxy now records accessors and data defines through the assignment\npath, rejects a getter-only write of its own value, and ignores the delete of\na hidden field it wrote. The flat tracker now compares a hidden object field\nby contents.\n\nLocal-only direct writes are compared with a local-only Collection whose\nhandlers resolve, across mixed multi-key batches, failing batches, schema\nrejection, and every fallback for insert, update, and delete. Mutants that\nignore handler types, drop the pending or persisting check, drop the whole\ntransaction check, run before the ambient branch, write only the first\nmutation, or never complete the transaction fail 4, 4, 4, 8, 12, 1, and 9\ntests.\n\nTwo shared conformance scenarios, `eq-filter-rows` and `eq-filter-peers`, run\nthe pooled path under React, Vue, Solid, Svelte, and Angular. A partition that\nignores a row's previous group fails both under every adapter. `eq-filter-peers` also checks the\nsource's public `subscriberCount`: an adapter that declares `pooledEqFilters`\nmust share one subscription for two queries on the same fields. Disabling pooling or using one partition per query fails it under React;\nbefore this check the first passed. Each adapter's driver declares\n`pooledEqFilters`.\n\nThe loss audit found that a partition released its source one second after\nits last listener, whatever `gcTime` its views had. A focused release-timing\ntest, `pooled-live-query-gc.test.ts`, now compares pooled and live-query\nCollection release times. A fixed delay failed 5 of 10 cases, a missing 50 ms\nfloor for unsubscribed queries 1, a last-`gcTime`-wins rule 1, and releasing\nat `gcTime` 0 2. Release timing is resource lifetime, so the publication\noracle does not observe it.\n\nPooling later admitted residual conjuncts: any `where` conjunct that reads\nonly the query's own row, beside at least one `eq`, is evaluated per view\nwith the compiler's evaluator. Peers may add `not(eq(g, literal))`, which the\nmodel evaluates itself, and a pinned history moves rows in and out of a view\nwithin one group. A view that ignores the residual, reports unfiltered\nentries, or sends a row entering or leaving it as an update fails the\npinned history and both campaigns. Hiding part of a group makes some\nearlier histories rarer, so in one full run the arrival-order,\nnormalization, delete-plus-insert, and dropped in-group update mutants\nescaped the random campaign; the fixed campaign and their pinned histories\nstill kill each. Peers' literals were weighted toward the normalized values\nto keep the normalization mutant in the fixed campaign.\n\n## Pooled live query oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass. The `eq` operand rules come from `src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts`; the pooled boundary and the terminal-error rule come from the live-query architecture document's pooled section and cleanup law. The opening prose lists the omissions. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedKeys` uses a local `eq` over plain values. Order, values, and status come from a live-query Collection, which compiles a D2 pipeline and does not use the partition. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. Contract, model, grammar, driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass. Reconstruction: every pinned history uses only domain values and step kinds. Ablation, run per axis on the campaigns with pinned cases skipped: without Dates, `NaN`, and `-0` the normalization mutant survives; without optimistic steps, frozen peers dropping optimistic rows survives; without mounts and unmounts, the terminal-at-creation mutant survives; without cleanup-restart, the status, termination, and both pending-cleanup mutants survive; without the second conjunct, first-field grouping survives. Range: at most four rows, three peers, eight steps, and ids 0 through 3; field values weight toward the literal so rows update within their group. Exclusion: an optimistic update to an equal value, an insert of an existing id, and an update or delete of a missing id are dropped. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. `createPooledLiveQuery` and `createLiveQueryObserver`, the adapter seam, run in wholesale and granular mode; the conformance scenarios run through React's `useLiveQuery`. Rows, keyed state, status, layout revision, and non-materialization are observed after every step, and granular changes are compared with a reference observer by type, key, value, and previous value, then replayed against the model. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. Thirteen mutants, classified above. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. Fixed seed `44_502_001`, an unseeded campaign, and a replay entry share one property and budget; the file is in `test:oracles`. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Pass. The model keeps source rows and, per mounted peer, the frozen keys at cleanup. The pinned cleanup history distinguishes a frozen peer from one mounted after the restart. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass. Equality partition, partition group, and pooled live query are glossary terms; a peer is one mounted pooled live query. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. `withOracleCleanup` releases observers, references, and the source and keeps the check failure. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Pass. The live-query Collection is the second formulation for order, values, and status. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record; the coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. Normalization is rejected by the Date history, arrival order by the key-order history, and following the source's status by the cleanup history. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. The claim is limited to the mock source's sync transactions and lifecycle. |\n\n## Flat change tracking oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass. The change-set rules come from `src/proxy.ts`'s `getChanges` contract: changed fields with their final value and deleted fields as `undefined`. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedChanges` folds the operations over a plain copy and does not import either tracker. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. Contract, model, grammar, driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass. Reconstruction: every pinned history uses domain values and operations. Ablation: removing `NaN`, `-0`, reverts, or deletions each loses a mutant. Range: one to three rows, three fields plus one added and one non-enumerable field, frozen or not, up to six operations per row including data and accessor defines, stored drafts, and a throw. Exclusion: none in generation; non-flat rows are rejected by the fallback witness. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. `withFlatChangeTracking`, `withArrayChangeTracking`, and `withChangeTracking` run the same callbacks; the result change sets are observed. `collection.update` selects between them. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. Thirteen mutants, classified above. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. Fixed seed `44_502_101`, an unseeded campaign, and a replay entry; the file is in `test:oracles`. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Not triggered. The model recomputes from the operations. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass. Draft, change set, and revert follow the proxy's terms. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Not triggered. The oracle holds no resources. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Pass. The draft proxy is the second formulation. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record; the coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. `!==` is rejected by the `NaN` history, `Object.is` by the `-0` history, and missing deletions by the deleted-field history. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. No provider is involved. |\n\n## Open work\n\n- Svelte's suite reads `@tanstack/db` from its built `dist`, so a mutant\n  must type-check and be rebuilt before Svelte can observe it.\n"}
